Q.ko_leader = "Donald Tusk";
Q.news_headline =
    "KO's own component ballots decide the consolidation";
!}

= October 2025 — A coalition is not yet one party

**Outgoing → incoming:** Donald Tusk's PO, Adam Szłapka's Nowoczesna and
Barbara Nowacka's Inicjatywa Polska enter as separately led organisations. The
historical consolidation creates one KO party under Tusk; PO's dominant
apparatus and pro-merger majorities in the two smaller parties support him,
while the Greens remain a separate ally.

**Why now:** years of campaigning under one coalition label have made three
memberships, structures, debts and sets of candidate rules an electoral
liability. The convention negotiates the terms, representation and treatment
of dissenters around the consolidation.

For Lewica, one disciplined liberal competitor is harder to out-organise. The
component parties will cast their own ballots from the cohesion and dissent
they accumulated before today. Lewica chooses one public stance; it cannot
decide their representation, programme, assets, dissolution or leader.

